/* book Worked example: Q: Compare the specific strength σult/ρ of three materials: 7075-T6 aluminium with σult = 570 MPa and ρ = 2810 kg/m³; Ti-6Al-4V with σult = 950 MPa and ρ = 4430 kg/m³; CFRP with σult = 1500 MPa and ρ = 1600 kg/m³. Give each answer in kN·m/kg and state which material wins on this ratio.
   1. Convert 7075-T6: σult = 570 MPa = 570 × 10⁶ Pa.
   2. Specific strength = (570 × 10⁶) / 2810 = 202 847 N·m/kg = 202.8 kN·m/kg ≈ 203 kN·m/kg.
   3. Convert Ti-6Al-4V: σult = 950 MPa = 950 × 10⁶ Pa.
   4. Specific strength = (950 × 10⁶) / 4430 = 214 447 N·m/kg = 214.4 kN·m/kg ≈ 214 kN·m/kg.
   5. Convert CFRP: σult = 1500 MPa = 1500 × 10⁶ Pa.
   6. Specific strength = (1500 × 10⁶) / 1600 = 937 500 N·m/kg = 937.5 kN·m/kg ≈ 938 kN·m/kg.
   7. Compare: CFRP is highest, then Ti-6Al-4V, then 7075-T6. CFRP still loses on compressive strength, temperature limit and repair.
   Answer: CFRP has the highest specific strength, about 938 kN·m/kg, against 214 kN·m/kg for Ti-6Al-4V and 203 kN·m/kg for 7075-T6; it still loses on compression, temperature and repair. */
